**First thing: measure, do not guess.** I would establish a baseline on Release builds with representative T-Drive inputs and typical `(epsilon, delta)`, then profile the inner loop before changing code.

You already emit `SIMPLIFY_CORE_MS` and have `timer.h` / Instruments signposts, but the `TIMER()` calls and `--time` wiring are not hooked into the stab loop. That is the first concrete step: instrument `get_longest_stab` and split time across `get_conv_from_grid`, `find_F`, `intersect`, and candidate maintenance, plus counters for `|P|`, alive candidates per step, and early deaths.

### What looks important from the code

The cost is almost entirely in `get_longest_stab` (`simplify.cpp`):

```29:51:simplify.cpp
    while (cur < int(stream.size())) {
        Gi = get_conv_from_grid(stream[cur], EPSILON, DELTA);
        for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
            if (dead[i]) continue;

            find_F(P[i], S[i], F[i]);

            if (!intersect(F[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
                dead[i] = true;
                dead_cnt++;
            }
        }
        // ...
    }
```

Roughly `O(n · Pn · (find_F + intersect))`, with `Pn = |boundary grid anchors|`.

| Priority | Area | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **`find_F` + `intersect`** | Called once per alive candidate per stream point. Dominant work. |
| 2 | **`Pn` / grid density** | Smaller `epsilon` densifies the disk grid and multiplies the inner loop. Parameter scaling often beats micro-opts. |
| 3 | **`wedge_gi_disjoint`** | Already written as a cheap prune to skip `find_F`+clip, but **never called**. High-value, low-risk if you validate it never drops live candidates. |
| 4 | **Allocations / copies** | `get_conv_from_grid` returns a new vector every step; `current_bbox()` allocates; lots of `Point` / `CGAL::to_double` traffic. Reuse buffers the way `clip` already does. |
| 5 | **Kernel / predicate cost** | EPIC + filtered orientation is correct but heavy on near-boundary cases. Hot path already mixes doubles; pushing more of `find_F`/`intersect` to plain doubles (with exact fallback only when needed) is the next layer. |

Less important for core runtime: GUI (`simplify_with_gui`), web JSON tracing, Frechet post-check, I/O. Hull caching in `get_conv_from_grid` is already a good win; I would not start there.

**Order of attack:** wire timing → confirm `find_F`/`intersect` dominate → enable and validate `wedge_gi_disjoint` → cut allocations in the stab loop → only then chase deeper geometry micro-opts or algorithmic changes to `Pn`.
